From 8df5df921c2e5fcafcdb16968345a4cfe6bb59a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 11:05:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix the flattened frog on the Android splash screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The launch theme applies @drawable/splash as android:background. A window
background that is a plain bitmap is STRETCHED to the window bounds with no
regard for aspect ratio, and the splash assets were full-screen compositions in
drawable-port-*/drawable-land-*. In locked landscape Android resolved the
portrait 1280x1920 asset and squashed it across a landscape window — reproducing
the reported screenshot almost exactly (see the before/after render: stretching
the portrait asset to a 2.06:1 window gives the same flattened frog).

Replaced with drawable/splash.xml, a layer-list:

  - a colour layer (@color/splashBackground, #0d1b2a) fills the window;
  - a bitmap layer drawn android:gravity="center", i.e. at its intrinsic size and
    never scaled, so it cannot be distorted whatever the window's aspect.

The bitmap is a new square splash_icon.png, 200dp in every density bucket
(200/300/400/600/800 px for mdpi..xxxhdpi), so it is the same physical size on
every screen. All eleven full-screen splash.png files and the now-empty
drawable-land-*/drawable-port-* folders are deleted, leaving exactly one `splash`
drawable and no orientation-qualified variants to be picked by surprise.

androidScaleType goes CENTER_CROP -> CENTER so the plugin's own splash — which
draws the same drawable in an ImageView once the activity starts — renders it
identically instead of rescaling it mid-launch. Its backgroundColor already
matches, so it fills the rest.

Documented the trap in CLAUDE.md: @capacitor/assets regenerates the full-screen
bitmaps and would reintroduce this.

Nothing in www/ changed — the web/PWA build never had a native splash, which is
why the GitHub Pages version looked right.
